      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beverly%2C_Alberta"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1913 Beverly became a village, and a year later it was incorporated as a town, marking rapid growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beverly%2C_Alberta"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Grand Trunk Pacific Railway built its own bridge downstream of Edmonton, bringing trains and coal shipments to Beverly.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beverly%2C_Alberta"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A park now sits on the former Beverly Mine site, preserving the town’s mining heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beverly%2C_Alberta"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Olde Towne Beverly Historical Society collects and shares stories of the community, keeping its history alive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
